#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5228a68-1e0a-5761-ac90-40fcab271214" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd13cf7-7215-5f4f-8a7c-38c0559334ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537244} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb969dc-7431-591a-8333-b0ef3b0cc412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537500} {:answer/id #uuid "ae62e6b6-9922-57f6-be87-76d55ca80e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537559} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a7b6ab-39e1-5f20-9b17-720ce6944c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537573} {:answer/id #uuid "f890beb3-8209-55e8-a5fa-269d219fee48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bd13cf7-7215-5f4f-8a7c-38c0559334ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537244} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fdc00ed-8996-53bf-b0b6-f7702bef817b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd69e9f7-e929-5fe2-b3bb-4bb36c948590" :question/math-academy-id "q-345553" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the third principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the third principal component is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5228a68-1e0a-5761-ac90-40fcab271214" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b599b3-e5fb-55be-8b4a-64cf19160660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537238} {:answer/id #uuid "89ae6ffb-c4b7-5567-8681-07205f0f211d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}{v}_{2}^{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537491} {:answer/id #uuid "c54575f9-512e-55ef-a0c7-e74be9d25000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}^{T}{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537610} {:answer/id #uuid "c6427590-8b6b-5345-bd73-92d79f31db5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537613} {:answer/id #uuid "feb49a45-3299-531f-989b-c646c0d9773a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feb49a45-3299-531f-989b-c646c0d9773a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537752} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a0ee814-fca1-58c8-8cc1-dbfe10fc9460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dcb3950f-e511-5b2b-8452-0fa93d40b2ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-345562" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {u}_{1} & ⋯ & {u}_{n} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & ⋯ & {v}_{m} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be an $n\\times m$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the second principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $m\\times m$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the second principal component is ${v}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dbfeaac-fc49-543d-b2b0-673b7dbb05d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "929589f9-2c1b-52ff-83d3-fa8d2f2ee07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191830} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c2a84c-85c4-59d7-962e-1ed8ea8a8e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191831} {:answer/id #uuid "0dac9873-d06e-599b-b424-744747db4a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191832} {:answer/id #uuid "43f7b588-fc22-5ed8-8e11-88e773839d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "97.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191833} {:answer/id #uuid "61cec5a4-6e98-53d4-b55b-1952616525f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "79.0%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dac9873-d06e-599b-b424-744747db4a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191832} :answer-field/id #uuid "705a4ca2-8121-50c8-969c-f904844cb959" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd0aa760-2231-5a4e-8f82-cb5ce0612345" :question/math-academy-id "q-345593" :question/problem "A scientist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each athlete among a group of athletes. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular athlete.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.5,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.1.\n$$\n\nThe scientist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.5)^{2}+(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =30.26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the scientist used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{29}{30.26} \\\\  & ≈0.958 \\\\  & =95.8%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dbfeaac-fc49-543d-b2b0-673b7dbb05d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49c18a90-d0ab-5db3-907e-9d6d19ce5251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537385} {:answer/id #uuid "534fc293-1b38-5991-9c40-35df9573a0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537402} {:answer/id #uuid "a003e2d0-0b5a-564f-bbea-c1c7cd17d282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "57.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537528} {:answer/id #uuid "dccecae2-fe53-5ef0-8dd7-5f7144995552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537671} {:answer/id #uuid "f21bf8a8-668c-531d-a786-a924eb2ffa9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dccecae2-fe53-5ef0-8dd7-5f7144995552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537671} :answer-field/id #uuid "f239ec3f-54ad-5f78-85ed-e887372ab958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a62d30b2-87c9-5394-8f0b-7a8e71495548" :question/math-academy-id "q-345594" :question/problem "An epidemiologist measured $4$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ for each disease affecting a specific population. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular individual.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=11,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=7,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe epidemiologist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $4\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(11)^{2}+(7)^{2}+(5)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =199\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the epidemiologist used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(11)^{2} \\\\  & =121.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{121}{199} \\\\  & ≈0.608 \\\\  & =60.8%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dbfeaac-fc49-543d-b2b0-673b7dbb05d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a343a9c-47b7-5434-8cc2-55123caf0ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537314} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0ed494-ccbd-5fee-87bf-c4772b8cd862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537352} {:answer/id #uuid "8458816f-8b48-5cde-9d4a-33d4e2eff2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537484} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ab9777-fef9-5b5d-be92-67582e5fc5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537725} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0758bb-92c5-56b2-82ce-f22f66450474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8458816f-8b48-5cde-9d4a-33d4e2eff2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537484} :answer-field/id #uuid "332d328e-144d-5da5-910d-9c9b357e23b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6923f3da-7346-5c88-a6fe-bd6d1873e88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345609" :question/problem "An economist measured $4$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ for each currency in a collection under devaluation. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular currency.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=12,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=7,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe economist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $4\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(12)^{2}+(7)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =203\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the economist used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(12)^{2} \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{144}{203} \\\\  & ≈0.709 \\\\  & =70.9%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
